Subject: Give an incremental parse the tree of a fresh parse

Give an incremental parse the tree of a fresh parse.

The copy of the runtime of the tree-sitter-cpp fork has this repair. The fork commit is
643c888.

After an edit, the incremental tree of a text must be the same as the
fresh tree of the text. At f0adac0, the incremental test of 23,768 edits
of 2,971 files had 11 different trees, and the undo test had 1
difference. The test of 43,560 edits of 1,452 snippets had 11 different
trees. The runtime had four causes.

- A fresh parse lexes the token after a node in the state after the last
  leaf of the node. Then it reduces the node with that token as the
  lookahead. The incremental parse pushed a reused node as one subtree,
  and it lexed the next token in the state after the node. The token
  kind, for example the `>` that closes a template argument list, and
  the keyword check of `inline` were then different. The parser now
  pushes the right spine of a reused node, until its last leaf.
- The parser reuses a node only when the stack has one version. The
  reductions for the node can make more versions. One version shifted
  the full node, and the versions did not merge where a fresh parse
  merges them. With more than one version, the parser now shifts the
  first leaf of the node.
- A node was fragile only when its first or last child was fragile. A
  child in the middle can come from more than one version. The order and
  the merges of those versions are related to the versions outside the
  node. Each fragile child now makes its parent fragile.
- A node with an ERROR or a MISSING node in a child in the middle was
  reusable. Error recovery uses the stack outside the node. The parser
  now parses such a node again.

These changes apply only to a parse with an old tree. The tree hashes of
the 329,387 corpus files and of the 7,646 compiler test files do not
change.

With AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, the incremental
test of the files, the undo test, and the test of the snippets give 0
differences. The 25 saved cases give the same trees. The unit tests in
bindings/rust/lib.rs hold five reduced cases.

The repair is not specific to a language. It has four parts that upstream can accept one at a time:

- A node with an error cost is not reused as one subtree
- A node with a fragile child becomes fragile
- A reused node that the parser shifts goes onto the stack as the subtrees of its right spine
- A reused node with more than one stack version breaks down to its first leaf

Risk for upstream: medium, because of the size.

The patch applies to the upstream tag v0.27.0, after the patches with a smaller number. The
series compiles with `-Wall -Wextra` and no warning.

The comments hold the marker "(tree-sitter-cpp fork)". Remove the marker before you send the
patch upstream.

